WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.660
The pancreas makes digestive enzymes and, in islets, a hormone.

2
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.180
By 1920, extracts lowered blood sugar, but none was safe for patients.

3
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:22.080
In November 1920, Frederick Banting took an idea to John Macleod, a Toronto

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.080
professor. Tie off the pancreatic duct in dogs.

5
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.920
The enzyme-making tissue wastes away.

6
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.940
Extract what is left.

7
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:36.483
Macleod gave space, dogs, and two student assistants.

8
00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:38.723
One was Charles Best.

9
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:46.343
In summer 1921, Banting and Best made extracts that lowered blood sugar in diabetic

10
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:54.343
dogs. On 11 January 1922, their extract went into Leonard Thompson, then in his

11
00:00:54.343 --> 00:00:59.163
early teens. His blood sugar fell a little, and an abscess formed.

12
00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:02.537
The extract was too impure.

13
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:06.937
James Collip raised the alcohol concentration in steps.

14
00:01:07.897 --> 00:01:12.257
Impurities dropped out of solution, while the active substance stayed in.

15
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:14.877
He tested each batch on rabbits.

16
00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:18.797
It went into the same boy on 23 January.

17
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:22.157
His blood sugar fell by about three quarters.

18
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:27.727
The 1923 Nobel Prize named Banting and Macleod.

19
00:01:28.447 --> 00:01:32.407
Banting shared his prize money with Best, Macleod with Collip.

20
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.727
The two-name story misses half the people.

21
00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:41.087
The receptor was not known to be a tyrosine kinase until 1982.

22
00:01:42.147 --> 00:01:45.147
The drug came first, and the mechanism came later.
